:: The Crawl [event mom_quest] {"position":"4525,1075","size":"100,100"}
/* Quest "The Crawl" (mom_crawl), STAGE1_PROPOSAL.md 5.6. The Friday after The Silence. The office empties out early, and she
   stays. She apologizes; I don't look up, tell her I didn't miss her for a second and let her know she's no use to me. She
   changes in the bathroom into the dress from her cousin's wedding (The Booze Night: she was almost sure it still fits) and
   crawls to my desk across the empty office. I make her take her line back out loud and ask for it nicely. Her first
   blowjob, and I humiliate her the whole way through. At the end I tell her that from now on she brings my coffee in the
   morning and does this with it, make her thank me for it, and send her back to the bathroom the way she came, on her knees.
   No device on her tonight; only the house-wide guilt nudge from Not Enough, all week.
   The quest's set unlocks the morning routine ($mom.flags.morningRoutine, passage Morning Routine). This scene switches on the meal
   crawl_thank_you: the next morning she thanks me for letting her make it up, at breakfast, in front of $sister.callName. */
<<silent>><<SetBackground $locations[14].img>><</silent>>
By five on a Friday, the office is a ghost town. $ethan.name left at four with a wink and the words "long weekend, boss, I can feel it in my bones", though it isn't one. $brian.name left at four-fifteen because $ethan.name left. $darius.name left at four-thirty, exactly, with his notebook under his arm. $sister.callName left with him, because her seminar starts at six.

$mom.callName didn't leave.

She's at her desk with her coat on, and she's had it on for forty minutes without typing a thing in the last thirty. Every few minutes she looks over at me, and every time she does, I'm looking at my screen.

It's been a week of her trying, in every small way she could think of, and of me walking past her in the kitchen like she was a piece of furniture. I haven't said a single word to her that wasn't about a client.

<<linkreplace "Keep working">>
At five-twenty she gets up. She takes her coat off, folds it over the back of her chair, and walks over to my desk. She stops a few feet away, the way she did in my room the night I sent her away, and stands there with her hands clasped in front of her.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $mom, text: "Can I talk to you for a minute, honey? You don't have to say anything, I just..." },
{ text: "I keep reading the email on my screen. I've read it three times." },
{ who: $mom, text: "I'm sorry about that night. About what I said to you." },
{ who: $mom, text: "I've been trying to figure out all week why I said it, because I've done everything you ever asked me to, everything, and then I went and drew this line, as if I was... as if I still got to decide something. And then you stopped needing me, and it's been the worst week I've had since your $dad.relation left. Worse, really, because back then at least I still had you." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

Her voice cracks on the last part, and she stops and breathes and steadies it, because she doesn't want to cry at me. She wants to apologize properly.

<<linkreplace "Don't look up">>
I let her stand there. I finish the sentence I'm typing, and then the one after it, and only then do I answer her, without taking my eyes off the screen.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $player, text: "Okay." },
{ text: "That's all. I scroll down." },
{ who: $mom, text: "Okay?" },
{ who: $player, text: "You're sorry, I heard you. What do you want now, a hug? A card?" },
{ who: $mom, text: "No, honey, I only meant..." },
{ who: $player, text: "The worst week since $dad.callName left. Wow. You want to know what my week was like? I got more done than I have in a month, and I slept like a baby every single night. I didn't miss you once. Honestly, I barely noticed you were gone." },
{ text: "That one lands. I can hear it land." },
{ who: $player, text: "So if that's everything, I'd like to finish this contract before I go home. You could have done it for me, actually, back when you were still some use to me." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

It's cruel, and I mean every word of it. The best part is that most of it is even true. I can feel her standing there taking it, and I can hear her breathing go ragged, and I keep scrolling. I don't have to look at her to know how this ends. I've known since Monday.

And then, very quietly:

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $mom, text: "Give me five minutes, please? Just don't go anywhere, honey." },
{ who: $player, text: "Five. I'm not sitting here all night waiting on you." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

She picks up her purse from her desk and walks to the bathroom at the far end of the office. The door closes. I hear the lock.

<<linkreplace "Wait five minutes">>
It's closer to ten. I finish the contract, which I didn't need to finish. The office has gone blue with dusk, and nobody's switched the lights on except the strip over my desk.

I keep thinking about all the people I've waited on in my life: the café chain that stopped returning my calls, $manager.callName deciding every Friday whether I'd get my shifts and making sure I saw him take his time over it. I spent years asking for things and being grateful for the scraps. This week I didn't ask her for a single thing. I ignored her, I left $company.prototype.name humming on low in a drawer at home, and she lay awake every night and took her own line apart for me, and now she's locked in my office bathroom getting ready to give me whatever she thinks will make me look at her again.

The bathroom door opens.

She's wearing a dress I've never seen. Dark green, silky, with thin straps and a neckline that goes lower than anything she owns. It was made for a younger woman at a wedding twenty years ago, and it fits her the way it was supposed to fit her then, and maybe a little better. Her hair is down, her lipstick is fresh and darker than anything she wears to work, and her feet are bare. She's left her heels in the bathroom.

It's the dress from her cousin's wedding, the one she told me about on the couch with half a bottle of wine in her. She was almost sure it still fit, and apparently she's checked.

/* MEDIA: images/characters/mom/events/the_crawl/dress.jpg | Mom in the doorway of the office bathroom in a dark green silk slip dress, barefoot, hair down, dark lipstick, the office dim and blue behind her */

<<linkreplace "Look at her">>
She doesn't walk over. She stands there and waits for me to look at her, all of her, and I take my time about it, the way you'd look over a used car somebody is a little too eager to sell you. Then she gets down onto her knees on the office carpet.

And she crawls.

All the way across the main room: past $ethan.name's desk with the noodle drawer, past Gerald the fern, past the coffee machine, slowly, on her hands and knees in a dress she wore at a wedding, with her eyes on me the whole way. The straps slip off one shoulder. She doesn't fix it. The carpet is going to leave marks on her knees, and she'll have to wear pants on Monday, and she knows it.

I don't get up, and I don't help her. I lean back in my chair and let her come the whole way.

/* MEDIA: images/characters/mom/events/the_crawl/crawl.mp4 | Mom crawling across the dim office toward the camera on hands and knees, dress slipping off one shoulder, eyes up */

She stops at my chair. She kneels up between my knees and puts her hands flat on my thighs, and looks up at me.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $mom, text: "I want to make it up to you, honey. Please let me." },
{ who: $player, text: "Funny. A week ago you sat on my bed and told me there were things a $mom.relation doesn't do. Go on, say it again. I want to hear how it sounds from down there." },
{ text: "Her face goes red right up to her hairline. She opens her mouth, and nothing comes out of it." },
{ who: $mom, text: "I know what I said." },
{ who: $player, text: "So what happened to it? Seven days of nobody saying good morning to you, and your big line just got up and walked off?" },
{ text: "She swallows. Her hands find my belt. They're perfectly steady." },
{ who: $mom, text: "I don't think I ever meant it." },
{ who: $player, text: "No, you didn't. You wanted me to chase you, and I didn't, and now look where you are. Ask me nicely." },
{ who: $mom, text: "Please, honey. Please let me." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

<<linkreplace "Let her">>
She's slow at first, careful, like she's doing something delicate and doesn't want to break it. Her lipstick leaves a ring. She makes a small surprised sound, at herself more than anything, and then she closes her eyes and stops being careful.

/* MEDIA: images/characters/mom/events/the_crawl/bj_0.mp4 | Mom kneeling between his knees at the desk, the desk lamp the only light */

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $player, text: "Look at you. All that crying in the shower over your precious line, and you crawled right over it on your hands and knees." },
{ text: "She whimpers around me. Her fingers dig into my thighs." },
{ who: $player, text: "You know what you looked like, coming across the carpet like that? Like a dog that got left home alone all week and just heard the car in the driveway." },
{ who: $player, text: "Slower. You made me wait a whole week for this, you don't get to rush it now." },
{ text: "She slows down at once. She doesn't need to be told twice." },
{ who: $player, text: "There you go. That's what you're good for, $mom.callName, all those rules of yours were never worth anything. Your line lasted exactly as long as it took me to stop looking at you." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

I can see every word go into her. Her face flushes, her eyes squeeze shut, and she takes me deeper, as if she could make up for each one of them. She doesn't argue with any of it. She only works harder, the way she does at everything, the way she's always done everything for me, and the meaner I get, the harder she works.

/* MEDIA: images/characters/mom/events/the_crawl/bj_1.mp4 | Closer, faster, his hand in her hair */

I put my hand in her hair and hold her where I want her. She leans into it like a cat.

When I come I keep her there, and she doesn't try to pull away. She stays with her eyes closed and swallows, breathing hard through her nose, until I let go of her hair. Then she sits back on her heels and wipes her mouth with the back of her hand. Her lipstick is everywhere. She doesn't seem to care.

<<linkreplace "Tell her how it's going to be">>
<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ text: "She opens her mouth to say something, and I get there first." },
{ who: $player, text: "Don't. I don't need a speech. Here's how it works from now on: you bring my coffee up in the morning." },
{ who: $mom, text: "But I already bring your coffee in the morning, sweetie." },
{ who: $player, text: "I know. And from now on you do this with it, before I'm even out of bed. You don't knock, you don't ask, and you never stand next to my bed again telling me where your lines are." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

She looks up at me from the floor. Her mascara has run a little at the corners, and both straps are off her shoulders now, and either she hasn't noticed or she's stopped caring about straps.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $mom, text: "Every morning?" },
{ who: $player, text: "Every morning, unless I tell you otherwise. And you're going to thank me for it." },
{ text: "There's no fight in her face at all. What's there looks almost like relief." },
{ who: $mom, text: "Okay. Yes, honey. Thank you. I can do that." },
{ who: $player, text: "Good. Now go fix your face, you look like a mess. And you came over here on your knees, so you can go back the same way." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

I pat her cheek twice with two fingers, the way you'd pat a dog that finally brought the stick back, and that's all it takes. She closes her eyes and leans her face into my hand and lets out a long, slow breath, the kind you let out at the end of a week like the one she's had. As far as she's concerned, the week is over and I need her again. She can think that if it helps her sleep.

She crawls back. She goes the whole way on her hands and knees, past the coffee machine and past $ethan.name's desk, with the green dress hanging off her, to the bathroom where her heels and her work clothes are waiting. Halfway there she stops at Gerald the fern, still on her knees, and straightens one of his leaves.

I watch her until the bathroom door closes behind her. Then I pull the contract up again, because there's a typo in the second paragraph and now I have time for it.

